OpenSSH is not a library and as such it is not possible to call its internal
API functions. Instead, this shim file will include the C files themselves and
define some functions that would be too onerous to include in the acvp-parser.
These functions intentionally exit with -EOPNOTSUPP to ensure that they are not
called at runtime.

To use this shim file, the OpenSSH source needs to be available *and* configured
for the machine you are testing (referenced in backends.mk). Moreover, OpenSSL
must be available. For example:
[joachim@joachim-xps openssh-9.6p1]$ ./configure
...
OpenSSH has been configured with the following options:
                     User binaries: /usr/local/bin
                   System binaries: /usr/local/sbin
               Configuration files: /usr/local/etc
                   Askpass program: /usr/local/libexec/ssh-askpass
                      Manual pages: /usr/local/share/man/manX
                          PID file: /var/run
  Privilege separation chroot path: /var/empty
            sshd default user PATH: /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/local/bin
                    Manpage format: doc
                       PAM support: no
                   OSF SIA support: no
                 KerberosV support: no
                   SELinux support: no
                   libedit support: no
                   libldns support: no
  Solaris process contract support: no
           Solaris project support: no
         Solaris privilege support: no
